urses"}, {"id": "polites-word3", "translation": "as"}, {"id": "polites-word4", "translation": "They hurl a stone at the citizens in doubt"}, {"id": "polites-word5", "translation": "Lift up glory for these"}, {"id": "polites-word6", "translation": "Or being just with citizen men"}, {"id": "polites-word7", "translation": "fellow citizens"}, {"id": "polites-word8", "translation": "They did many things"}, {"id": "polites-word9", "translation": "fear"}, {"id": "polites-word10", "translation": "dear"}, {"id": "polites-word11", "translation": "the one who untimely keeps the city"}, {"id": "polites-word12", "translation": "men"}, {"id": "polites-word13", "translation": "To the citizens all things"}, {"id": "polites-word14", "translation": "I will try to rule"}, {"id": "polites-word15", "translation": "citizens"}, {"id": "polites-word16", "translation": "without"}, {"id": "polites-word17", "translation": "When their own citizens do not embitter the laws"}, {"id": "polites-word18", "translation": "I am laughed at"}, {"id": "polites-word19", "translation": "Time will be more honored"}, {"id": "polites-word20", "translation": "to these"}, {"id": "polites-word21", "translation": "black blood"}, {"id": "polites-word22", "translation": "May the thought of the good be good"}, {"id": "polites-word23", "translation": "all"}, {"id": "polites-word24", "translation": "gods"}, {"id": "polites-word25", "translation": "unharmed"}, {"id": "polites-word26", "translation": "Sic Bipstorius: πολήταις [citizens] M. πολίταις [citizens] in the apograph"}, {"id": "pollakis-orth1", "translation": "often"}, {"id": "pollakis-def1", "translation": "often."}, {"id": "pollakis-def2", "translation": "Epic and lyric form"}, {"id": "pollakis-note1", "translation": "Τοιάδ᾽ ἐξ ἀνδρῶν ὀνείδη [Such reproaches from men]"}, {"id": "pollakis-note2", "translation": "Πολ-λάκις πατρὸς κατ᾽ ἀνδρῶνας εὐτραπέζους [often through the hospitable halls of my father]"}, {"id": "pollakis-note3", "translation": "the tragedians sometimes used it in lyric passages when the meter demanded, once Sophocles (Ph. 1456), twice Aeschylus in dochmiac verses,"}, {"id": "pollakis-note4", "translation": "Πολλάκι δ᾽ ἐν κακοῖσι τὸν ἀμάχανον [And often in troubles the helpless one]"}, {"id": "pollakis-note5", "translation": "Πολλάκι δ᾽ ἐμπίτνω ξὺν λακίδι [And often I fall with a tattered garment]"}, {"id": "pollakis-quote1", "translation": "often hearing of evils"}, {"id": "pollakis-quote2", "translation": "sang"}, {"id": "pollakis-quote3", "translation": "sets upright"}, {"id": "pollakis-quote4", "translation": "with linen"}, {"id": "pollakis-word1", "translation": "Such reproaches from men"}]}